Q: How to convert a .txt file of 0&#39;s and 1&#39;s to a .bin? A: <p>Normally when we work with bit data. If we have bit counter for count bit is full to buffer or not (not count bits in buffer). it's will make program easier.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;sstream&gt;
#include &lt;cstdint&gt;


int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {

    constexpr auto input = R&quot;(00110010 10 11 011
11110000 10 1   0 0 0  0 0 0 111
0111 001 01 01 0 0 0111 1011)&quot;;//assume to your data in file

    std::istringstream is{ input };// assume to std::ifsream

    std::string str_buf;
    uint32_t bit_buf = 0;
    int underflow = 32;//counter

    while (is &gt;&gt; str_buf) {
        for (char c: str_buf) {
            switch (c) {
            case '0':
                underflow--;
                break;
            case '1':
                bit_buf |= 1 &lt;&lt; underflow--;
                break;
            default:
                std::cerr &lt;&lt; &quot;skip or do something\n&quot;;
                break;
            }

            if (underflow == 0) {
                /*
                    flush/write to file here and reset bit buf, counter
                */
                underflow = 32; 
                bit_buf = 0;
            }
        }

    }
    if (underflow &lt; 32) {
        /*
            flush/write remain
        */
    }

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Fluent assertion for OR condition A: <p>You can always use <code>try..catch</code> to achieve <code>OR</code> condition with assertions:</p>
<pre><code>response?.Results.Should().AllSatisfy(actual =&gt;
{
    actual.Id.Should().Be(expected.Id);
    try
    {
        actual.Status.Should().Contain(expected.Status);
    }
    catch (AssertionException)
    {
        actual.Status.Should().BeOneOf(expected.Status.Split(&quot; &quot;));
    }
});
</code></pre>
<p>It is not pretty, but does the job right and also follows testing mindset I guess :)</p>
